**Definition 2.5 (complete source-family error).** Canonical output is the
classical–quantum direct sum of terminal live success and exact first
failure time/mode with $f$. An acquisition abort or rejection absent from
the ideal output occupies a separate sector and counts as error. For two
normalized outputs let

$$
\operatorname{dist}(\omega,\eta)=\tfrac12\|\omega-\eta\|_{1,\rm cq}.
\tag{AC.4}
$$

The norm is the sum/integral of trace norms over the classical sectors.
For the canonical density representation it is the terminal block norm
plus $\sum_{i=0}^4\int_0^L\|\omega_i(t)-\eta_i(t)\|_1dt$.
For other measures use any common dominating scalar measure; singular
events and aborts remain included. Exact time is a measurable variable,
not an uncountable orthonormal Hilbert basis or a finite-bit register.

For each actual history the error is
$e_{x,\beta,m}=\operatorname{dist}(\Phi(\rho_{x,\beta,m}),
\Omega_{x,\beta,m})$, with its own prescribed ideal output $\Omega$.
This is comparison of source-indexed statistical experiments. It is not
substitution of different inputs into Instrument Stability's common-input
$d_{\rm op}$. A uniform complete-map/reference claim would imply these
source-family bounds, because it includes these inputs with trivial
reference. Retaining more actual joint fields can only strengthen the
requirement; forgetting fields gives a necessary lower test.

